[Music]  hello my name is michael crapon  i'm the co-founder of a think tank in  washington  called the stimson  center and i've worked on nuclear issues  for quite some time  for several decades  i've written a book about  the arms competition in arms control  it's called winning and losing the  nuclear piece  i say this by way of introduction  because i've been asked to talk to you  about that truth  about the nuclear threat  there are many truths  about the nuclear threat  but i'm going to give you some headlines  that most of us can agree on  at least it's hard to argue with them  and i'm going to headline them for you  in bold letters so the first bold  statement  is that the nuclear threat is real  it's not imaginary  it's all too real  video games and movies and social media  of varying kinds tend to make this  escapism  but i have trouble with escapism when it  comes to  nuclear danger which is very real  it's real because  two major powers  in the world  do not feel comfortable with  and would like to change the status quo  so that's dangerous  russia and china  would like a different  status quo  than the one that exists today  and there are dangers inherent in  changing the status quo  dangers are real because  there are border skirmishes  between nuclear-armed states  they happen  between india and pakistan between  india and china  so there are dangers in that  and danger exists in nuclear warfighting  plans  and doctrines for use  if you possess nuclear weapons you have  plans to use them  and those are very very dangerous  so here's my  second  headline in bold  while nuclear danger is real it is not  going away  not anytime soon  every state that possesses nuclear  weapons  is replacing old stuff  with newer stuff  nobody is standing pat  you've probably heard a fair amount  about some of the new stuff  these things called hypersonic  missiles  there are press reports that russia used  sung with conventional warheads  in the uh war against ukraine  but  these weapons  in my view are not  going  to play a very  outsized role in our nuclear future  and the reason is that the old stuff  works perfectly well  and the old stuff  also travels at hypersonic speeds  it's damn near impossible to stop the  old stuff  delivered by land-based missiles or by  submarines at sea  especially if the shooter  really wants to get through  has the money to get through  and has the technical expertise to get  through so these  hypersonics that you keep hearing about  and may well be worried about  they'll be part of our future but  they're going to be a niche  weapon the new stuff will just be better  versions of the old stuff for the most  part  nuclear danger is not going to go away  anytime soon  because the possessors  feel very comfortable  with their possessions  they feel very uncomfortable  with letting them go  you know  getting off the nuclear weapons habit  is different  than  stopping  smoking  you can go cold turkey  from  the nicotine habit  but you can't  go cold turkey with nuclear weapons  unless other folks join you  and this is a very complicated  very complicated orchestration  the diplomacy  has to be superb the timing has to be  right  to make great strides  and there have been great strides  but going to zero is a really hard  problem  here's my third  headline for you in bold  while the nuclear danger is real  and not going away  it's usually overblown  now  that may come  as a bit of a surprise  to some of you  but the nuclear danger is overblown  because both  deterrent strategists  and arms controllers  uh are in the business  of  accentuating these threats  and  don't get me wrong there's absolutely no  reason for complacency  this work has been my career because  it is dangerous stuff  and  it can be the ruination  of human  animal and plant life  so  it is worth worrying about  but  we've got history on our side  and i i'd like you to  take this thought away with you  whenever you're feeling extremely  anxious  about nuclear danger  these weapons were used to end a world  war  a world war in which somewhere between  50  and 75 million people lost their lives  these weapons have not been used in  battle ever since  we've gone three quarters of a century  without battlefield use  now think about that  they've been  around  125  000  nuclear weapons  manufactured  since  1945  not  one  has been used in warfare  what explains this  amazing  history  history that previous generations  found inconceivable  but found themselves relying upon  one war after another  one  god-awful crisis after another  one close call after another  nobody  no national leader  push the button  why  why  was it  plain dumb luck  was it uh divine intervention  i'm not discounting either one  deterrence is part of the answer  i'll grant deterrent strategists that  but it's only part of the answer  because  this business of if you strike me  i'm going to strike you back  only twice as hard  deterrence is dangerous  it's  purposefully dangerous otherwise it  doesn't deter  and so that's why we see  modifications  strengthening measures of deterrence  that sharpen threats  deterrence alone points toward use  it doesn't entirely explain  non-use  for three quarters of a century  for this we have arms control to think  deterrence  needs arms control to succeed  because arms control is about  reassurance  it's not about sharpening threats  it's about opening lines of  communication  the first lines of real communication  between washington and moscow  in the cold war  happened through arms control  through negotiations  which were extremely awkward at the  beginning  arms control isn't just about  communications  it's about stopping  dangerous military practices  and the very first dangerous military  practice  that arms control stopped  was nuclear testing in the atmosphere  it was  there were so many tests  in the atmosphere  at test sites  in colonial possessions  in out of the way places not in my  backyard  north africa  the south pacific  australia  kazakhstan  so many other places  soviet union in the united states  people were severely punished by nuclear  testing and it stopped  didn't stop because of deterrence  if deterrence had held swear we would  have kept testing  it stopped because of arms control  and one agreement led to the next  because that is the  is the ideal  outcome  or process  i should say  of arms control you stop nuclear testing  in the atmosphere  you stop it above certain yields  underground  you arrange for verification  monitoring  mechanisms  so you can tell the yields of  underground tests  then you stop the tests all together  which happened in 1996.  now you're going to hear all kinds of  concerns that maybe somebody cheated  here or somebody cheated near  at extremely low yields  yields so low they don't even shake  seismometers  nuclear  testing amongst all  major and regional powers  has stopped  and has stopped for over two decades  there's one outlier left  that's north korea  to continue watching this video click  the link in the top left or in the  description below or visit iai.tv for  more debates and talks from the world's  leading thinkers on today's biggest  ideas

